{"type":"text-delta","id":"0","delta":"## Summary of Conversation\n\n### Task Context\nThe user shared a very long reference document about web development and software engineering (duplicated 5 times for \"redundancy\"). They asked a series of questions about different topics covered in the documentation.\n\n### Conversation Flow\n1. **React vs Vue.js State Management**: I explained React's hooks (useState, useReducer, useContext) and Redux vs Vue's Composition API (ref, computed, watch) and Pinia (defineStore with state, getters, actions).\n\n2. **Docker and Kubernetes**: I explained Docker's containerization (Dockerfile, Docker Compose) and Kubernetes orchestration (Deployments, Services, Ingress), plus how they work together in a development-to-production workflow.\n\n3. **Testing Strategies**: I covered the differences between Unit Testing (Jest - isolated functions, fast), Integration Testing (supertest - API endpoints, service communication), and E2E Testing (Playwright - real user flows in browsers), plus the testing pyramid concept.\n\n4. **SQL vs NoSQL Databases**: I explained SQL databases (DDL, DML, JOINs, window functions, CTEs, indexing) vs NoSQL options (MongoDB for documents, Redis for key-value/caching, Neo4j for graphs), and when to use each.\n\n5. **Current Question**: The user asked me to summarize key algorithms and data structures from the documentation. This is the pending question I need to answer.\n\n### Pending Response Needed\nI need to provide a comprehensive summary of algorithms and data structures from the documentation, covering:\n- **Data Structures**: Arrays, Linked Lists, Stacks, Queues, Priority Queues, Binary Search Trees, Hash Tables, Graphs\n- **Sorting Algorithms**: Quick Sort, Merge Sort, Heap Sort\n- **Searching Algorithms**: Binary Search (and variants)\n- **Dynamic Programming**: Fibonacci, LCS, Knapsack, Coin Change, Edit Distance\n- **Big O Notation**: Common complexities from O(1) to O(n!)\n\n### Key Pattern\nThe user asks broad questions about sections of the documentation and expects organized, structured answers that summarize the key concepts. They've been asking follow-up questions sequentially through the major sections. This was their \"final\" question in the series."}
